  "analysis": [
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "answer": "Current law caps residential rent increases at 5 percent in any 12-month period for units over 15 years old. The measure repeals this limit entirely, allowing landlords to raise rents by any amount as long as they provide 60 days' written notice.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "answer": "Landlords and property owners benefit by gaining the ability to set rents at market rates without restriction. Renters bear the cost of potentially higher housing expenses and face a greater risk of displacement due to unaffordable rent hikes.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "answer": "Landlords may raise rents more frequently or by larger amounts. In the long term, removing rent caps might encourage developers to build more residential units and landlords to better maintain existing properties, though current tenants facing steep increases may be forced to relocate.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "answer": "The measure addresses the argument that rent control artificially limits returns on property investment, disincentivizes maintenance, and suppresses new construction. The mechanism allows free-market pricing. However, removing the cap recreates the problem of renters facing sudden, unaffordable rent hikes and housing instability.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "answer": "Landlords and property owners gain discretion and power over setting rental prices.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q7",
      "answer": "The economic and social effects of rent control are empirically disputed. Some researchers find that rent control successfully prevents the displacement of current tenants and provides crucial short-term housing stability. Conversely, other researchers find that rent caps reduce the overall supply and quality of housing by discouraging investment and new construction, ultimately harming future renters.",
      "disputed": true
    }
  ],
